introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do machines manage chaos and control the physical world?"
  overview: "This unit focuses on two critical HL concepts: <strong>Concurrency</strong> (how OSs manage multiple tasks without crashing) and <strong>Control</strong> (how computers sense and change the physical world). You will master the logic of <strong>Feedback Loops</strong> and understand the dangers of <strong>Deadlock</strong>."
  journey: "You'll start by looking at the Operating System's struggle to manage competing resources (Multitasking). Then you'll move to physical computing, defining the Input-Process-Output cycle of <strong>Sensors and Actuators</strong>, determining when to use Open-Loop vs Closed-Loop logic."

concepts:
  - title: "Multitasking & Deadlock"
    icon: "fa-layer-group"
    content: |
      <p>When multiple programs want the same resource, chaos can ensue.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Resource Contention:</strong> Processes fighting for CPU/Memory.</li>
          <li><strong>Deadlock:</strong> A standstill where Process A waits for B, and B waits for A. (The "Dining Philosophers" problem).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Control Systems Structure"
    icon: "fa-robot"
    content: |
      <p>Automated systems follow a strict Hardware-Software loop:</p>
      <ol>
          <li><strong>Sensor:</strong> Detects physical data (Heat, Light).</li>
          <li><strong>Transducer:</strong> Converts physics to electricity (ADC).</li>
          <li><strong>Controller:</strong> The CPU/Logic that makes a decision.</li>
          <li><strong>Actuator:</strong> The muscle that creates movement/change (Motor, Heater).</li>
      </ol>

  - title: "Feedback Loops"
    icon: "fa-rotate"
    content: |
      <p>Does the system know if it succeeded?</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Open-Loop:</strong> Input -> Process -> Output. (No checking. e.g., Cheap Toaster, Sprinkler).</li>
          <li><strong>Closed-Loop:</strong> Input -> Process -> Output -> <strong>Feedback</strong> -> Adjust. (Self-correcting. e.g., Thermostat, Cruise Control).</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Multitasking"
    definition: "The ability of an OS to execute more than one task simultaneously (via time-slicing)."
  - term: "Deadlock"
    definition: "A situation where two or more processes are unable to proceed because each is waiting for the other to release a resource."
  - term: "Sensor"
    definition: "A device that detects or measures a physical property and records, indicates, or otherwise responds to it."
  - term: "Actuator"
    definition: "A component of a machine that is responsible for moving and controlling a mechanism or system."
  - term: "Transducer"
    definition: "A device that converts variations in a physical quantity (like pressure) into an electrical signal (or vice versa)."
  - term: "Feedback"
    definition: "Information about the output of a system that is returned to the input to modify the next action."
  - term: "Open-Loop System"
    definition: "A control system where the output has no influence or effect on the control action."
  - term: "Closed-Loop System"
    definition: "A control system that uses feedback to maintain a desired output."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Deadlock is just Lag"
    correction: "Thinking the computer is just slow. Deadlock is a fatal logic freeze. Unless the OS kills a process, it will wait FOREVER."
  - mistake: "Sensors do the work"
    correction: "Thinking the thermometer heats the room. The Sensor MEASURES. The Actuator (Heater) DOES the work."
  - mistake: "Open-Loop is 'Bad'"
    correction: "Assuming Closed-Loop is always better. Open-loop is simpler and cheaper. Your toaster doesn't need a laser-scan of the bread; a timer is enough."

